meta-edf-ptest-tests
===================

Yocto/OpenEmbedded layer providing EDF hardware and system sanity tests through
ptest. It includes a sanity-test package group, shared Python utilities and
HTML reporting in ptestlib.

Dependencies and compatibility
==============================

The layer declares a dependency on OpenEmbedded Core (core) and compatibility
with the wrynose release series in conf/layer.conf. Its BitBake collection name
is edf-ptest-coverage.

Use a configured Yocto build environment for your target board. Individual tests
also require the corresponding hardware, kernel drivers, and runtime tools.

Adding the layer and enabling ptest
==================================

From an initialized build environment, add this checkout:

    bitbake-layers add-layer /path/to/meta-edf-ptest-tests


Building packages and images
===========================

Select one of the following machines in your configured Yocto build environment:

* amd-cortexa53-common
* amd-cortexa53-mali-common
* amd-cortexa72-common
* amd-cortexa78-common
* amd-cortexa78-mali-common

Build the sanity-test package group and its dependencies with MACHINE set to
your selected machine. For example:

    MACHINE=amd-cortexa78-mali-common bitbake packagegroup-edf-ptest-sanity-coverage

Build your target image after adding the configuration above:

    MACHINE=amd-cortexa78-mali-common bitbake <image-recipe>

When RPM packaging is enabled, packages are deployed under
${TMPDIR}/deploy/rpm/<package-architecture>/. Architecture directories depend on
build configuration; test recipes and ptestlib currently use the all package
architecture. Other package formats use their corresponding deploy directories.
Building the package group alone does not create a target image.
The package group installs the test packages, ptest-runner, and ptestlib.
The ptest-pkgs image feature includes the associated test payload packages.

Available sanity tests
======================

The package group includes tests for:

* CAN, DMA, Ethernet connectivity, GPIO, and I2C
* Memory, RTC, SD, SPI, SPI JFFS2, UART, USB, and watchdog
* Docker, UFS configuration, kernel module development, and PL GPIO

The authoritative package list is in
recipes-core/packagegroups/packagegroup-edf-ptest-sanity-coverage.bb.

Running tests on the target
===========================

List installed ptests:

    ptest-runner -l

Run all installed ptests:

    ptest-runner

Run selected tests using names from the listing:

    ptest-runner urvf-gpio-basic-test urvf-rtc-basic-test

Run hardware tests with the privileges and board setup required by their test
scripts. Review scripts before running them: some modify device state or
exercise storage and watchdogs.

Tests using ptestlib write logs to /tmp/test_logs and reports to
/tmp/test_reports by default. Override these directories before running tests:

    export LOG_FOLDER=/path/to/test_logs
    export REPORT_FOLDER=/path/to/test_reports

Generate an HTML report from an existing log:

    urvf_reporter.py pass /path/to/test.log test_name -o /path/to/report.html

Use pass or fail for the status argument.

Adding a test
=============

Use an existing test, such as recipes-bsp/urvf-gpio-basic-test/, as a reference
and create a new directory under recipes-bsp/ or recipes-support/:

    urvf-example-basic-test/
        urvf-example-basic-test.bb
        files/ptest/run-ptest
        files/ptest/test_example_basic.py

In the recipe, inherit ptest, list the test files in SRC_URI, and install them
into ${PTEST_PATH} in do_install_ptest(). Add the runtime dependencies required
by the test. Update run-ptest to execute the new Python script and propagate
its exit status.

Implement the test logic and add the new package name to RDEPENDS:${PN} in
recipes-core/packagegroups/packagegroup-edf-ptest-sanity-coverage.bb. Build the
new recipe in a configured Yocto environment and verify it on the intended
target using ptest-runner.

Shared runtime helpers and the report generator are in
recipes-core/ptestlib/files/.

Licensing
=========

Layer scripts and shared Python utilities use the MIT license in COPYING.MIT.
Each shell and Python script carries an SPDX identifier, copyright notice, and
the full MIT license text. MIT recipes checksum a local script's license header;
the package group checksums the layer's COPYING.MIT file. When adding a test,
include the appropriate license headers and update LIC_FILES_CHKSUM to match
the referenced license text.
